            4. How to Choose: Decision Matrix by Use Case
          </h2>
          <p>
            The "best" garage floor coating is always the one that's best matched to your specific situation — your garage's use patterns, your climate, your experience level, and your budget. This decision matrix maps coating types to use cases to make the selection as straightforward as possible.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 mt-8">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">01</span> Daily Driver Garage (Warm Climate)
              </h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">You park one or two vehicles that are driven regularly, and summers are hot. Hot-tire pickup is your primary risk. <strong>Recommended: Rust-Oleum RockSolid Polycuramine.</strong> The hardness advantage directly addresses your most likely failure mode, the UV stability keeps the floor looking good in a sun-exposed space, and the price premium over standard epoxy is justified by the dramatically better performance under your actual conditions. Standard water-based epoxy will likely disappoint you within the first summer.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">02</span> Workshop or Tool-Heavy Garage
              </h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">You use the garage as a functional workspace — welding, woodworking, automotive repairs, metalworking. Chemical spills, dropped tools, and heavy rolling loads are common. <strong>Recommended: ArmorPoxy 100% Solids Epoxy.</strong> The thick film build, exceptional chemical resistance, and long service life make it the right tool for a genuinely demanding environment. The higher application difficulty is manageable for a mechanically capable DIYer, and the cost per year of service life is lower than any other option over a fifteen-year horizon.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black">03</span> Showroom or Car Collection Garage
              </h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Appearance matters as much as durability. The floor is frequently photographed, has significant lighting, and possibly direct sun exposure. UV stability and color retention are top priorities. <strong>Recommended: Rust-Oleum Polyaspartic Kit.</strong> The permanent UV stability, fast cure, and exceptional long-term gloss retention make it the clear choice for an appearance-first application. The higher cost is appropriate for a space where the floor is as much a design element as a functional surface.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
