        <!-- SECTION 5: Floor Prep -->
        <div id="floor-prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Preparing Your Garage Floor for Any Coating
          </h2>
          <p>
            Across every coating type discussed in this guide — from KILZ Over Armor to 100% solids epoxy — surface preparation is the single greatest determinant of how long the coating will last. An expert-level product applied over a poorly prepared surface will fail sooner than a budget product applied over perfectly prepared concrete. The chemistry of every coating type depends on a clean, sound, porous surface to achieve adequate adhesion, and no coating can chemically compensate for a compromised substrate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Moisture Testing — The Most Important Test Most DIYers Skip</h3>
          <p>
            Before any surface preparation begins, test for moisture vapor transmission. Tape a 24-inch square of heavy plastic sheeting to the bare floor, seal all edges with duct tape, and leave it for 24 hours. If moisture droplets form on the underside of the plastic, your concrete has active moisture vapor transmission — a condition that will cause any coating to delaminate, regardless of product quality or surface preparation effort. Moisture vapor transmission is common in older slabs, slabs on grade over poor drainage, and garages in high-humidity climates. Address the moisture source before applying any coating; do not attempt to coat over it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Degreasing — The Overlooked Step That Kills Adhesion</h3>
          <p>
            Oil contamination is the second most common cause of coating failure after moisture. Years of vehicle parking deposit motor oil, transmission fluid, power steering fluid, and brake fluid into the concrete surface — and these penetrate deep into the concrete matrix, not just the surface. Acid etching does not remove oil; it simply exposes oily concrete underneath. You must degrease the floor thoroughly before etching, and you may need to degrease multiple times on heavily contaminated sections. Apply a concentrated concrete degreaser, scrub with a stiff-bristle brush, allow dwell time per the product instructions, rinse thoroughly, and inspect. If the water still beads on the rinsed surface, there is still oil present and the degreasing step needs to be repeated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Acid Etching or Mechanical Grinding</h3>
          <p>
            Sound, clean concrete that has not been previously sealed or painted should be acid-etched before coating application. The included etching solution in most kits (or a diluted muriatic acid solution at 10:1 water-to-acid ratio) opens the concrete surface pores and creates a profile for the coating to mechanically bond into. Apply the solution evenly, allow it to react (you'll see light bubbling), scrub with a stiff brush, and rinse completely. After drying, perform the water-bead test: sprinkle water on the surface and confirm it soaks in immediately like water on a sponge. If water beads, the surface needs additional etching or mechanical preparation.
          </p>
          <p>
            For concrete that has been previously sealed, painted, or coated, acid etching is typically insufficient — a diamond-cup angle grinder or floor grinding machine is required to remove the existing surface treatment and expose fresh concrete. Applying a new coating over an old painted or sealed surface without mechanical removal is one of the most reliable ways to guarantee premature failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a detailed step-by-step application walkthrough covering surface prep through topcoat application, see our guide at <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">best-garage-floor-epoxy</a> — it covers the complete preparation and application process in full detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Crack Repair Before Coating</h3>
          <p>
            Every crack in the concrete wider than a hairline should be repaired before coating application. Hairline cracks (less than 1/16 inch) are typically stable and can be bridged by the coating. Larger cracks require filling with a concrete crack repair compound or flexible epoxy crack filler before the base coat is applied. Cracks that are growing or actively moving — caused by settling, root intrusion, or heave — need to be stabilized structurally before applying any surface coating. Applying a decorative floor coating over a structurally active crack simply transfers the crack pattern directly through the coating, often within months.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">The Preparation Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900 text-sm leading-relaxed">For every hour you spend applying coating, plan to spend two hours on surface preparation. The prep work is what determines whether you're still happy with your floor in year five, or recoating in year one. No coating technology can substitute for a properly prepared substrate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply polyaspartic over old epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, with conditions. If the existing epoxy coating is fully adhered, clean, and not peeling, you can apply a polyaspartic topcoat over it — but you must first scuff-sand the entire surface with 80-grit sandpaper or a floor buffer with sanding screens to create mechanical adhesion. Polyaspartic applied directly over smooth, glossy cured epoxy without sanding will delaminate. If the existing epoxy is peeling or was applied over a contaminated surface, remove it completely before applying any new coating. The integrity of the bond between polyaspartic and the substrate is only as strong as the weakest layer in the coating stack.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should you wait between coats of garage floor coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">The standard recommendation is to apply the second coat or topcoat within the <strong>recoat window</strong> specified by the manufacturer — typically 12–24 hours after the base coat for epoxy products. Applying within this window while the base coat is in its "green" (partially cured) state allows chemical bonding between coats, which produces much stronger inter-coat adhesion than waiting for the base coat to fully cure. If you miss the recoat window and the base coat has fully cured, you must scuff-sand the surface before applying the next coat to create mechanical adhesion. Always check the specific recoat window in the product instructions, as it varies by product chemistry and temperature.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a garage floor coating slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Plain epoxy and polyaspartic coatings have moderate slip resistance when dry but can be quite slippery when wet — particularly with smooth, high-gloss finishes. The two most effective ways to improve wet-surface traction are: broadcasting decorative vinyl chips at high density into the wet base coat (the chip texture significantly improves grip) or adding anti-slip aggregate (aluminum oxide or quartz sand) to the topcoat before application. Most manufacturers offer anti-slip additives specifically formulated for their products. For garage floors in climates where snow and water are regularly tracked in, adding an anti-slip aggregate to the topcoat is strongly recommended for safety.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you maintain a garage floor coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Garage floor coatings are low-maintenance but benefit from a few simple practices. Sweep or dust-mop regularly to prevent abrasive grit from accumulating under foot and tire traffic — grit acts like sandpaper on the coating surface and accelerates wear over time. Mop with a pH-neutral cleaner for periodic deep cleaning; avoid harsh acidic or alkaline degreasers that can dull or etch the surface finish. Clean up chemical spills promptly — particularly brake fluid, which is aggressive on most coatings. In winter, use a rubber-edged snow shovel or floor squeegee rather than a hard plastic shovel, which can scratch the surface. With these basic maintenance practices, a quality epoxy or polyaspartic coating will maintain its appearance and performance for years without requiring any significant intervention.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

