        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Garage Floor Coating Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garage floor coatings almost never fail because the product was bad. They fail because the slab was not ready for them.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Preparing With Acid Instead of a Grinder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid etching opens a slab a little; diamond grinding opens it properly and levels the surface at the same time. On a hard-troweled garage slab, etching frequently leaves the surface too closed for the coating to key into, and the floor delaminates under a hot tyre a year later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Testing for Vapour Transmission</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many garage slabs were poured without a vapour barrier underneath, and the moisture that moves up through them will push any film coating off. The plastic-sheet test takes 48 hours and a roll of tape, and it determines whether you need a moisture-mitigating primer before anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a One-Part Product for a Working Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One-part epoxy-fortified paints roll on easily and stand up to foot traffic, but hot tyres, dropped tools, and solvent spills go straight through them. If the garage sees actual use, a two-part epoxy or a polyaspartic is the category to shop in, whatever the price difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on It Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Walk-on dry and cure are weeks apart for most coatings. Parking a car on a floor that has not fully cured leaves permanent tyre marks and can lift the coating outright. Respect the manufacturer's full-cure time — usually five to seven days for epoxy — before the car comes back in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best <strong class="text-white">garage floor coating</strong> depends on what the floor has to survive. One-part acrylics and epoxy paints suit light-use garages on a budget. Two-part epoxies are the durable middle ground for most homeowners. Polyaspartics cost the most, cure fastest, and hold up best to UV and hot tyres.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Whichever you choose, the preparation decides the outcome. Grind rather than etch, run a moisture test before you buy anything, degrease every oil stain, and give the coating its full cure before the car returns. A mid-range product on a properly prepared slab beats a premium one on a poor slab, every time.
          </p>
